<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluating an Integral by parts-question 1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p></p><p><span id="docs-internal-guid-c4ff917c-7fff-8de2-5e7a-5a7a32781122">Evaluate \( \displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} dx\)</span><br></p>
<p>We have to employ Integration by parts technique to evaluate this integral. Recall the formula \[\int udv=uv-\int vdu\] </p>
<p>Applying this formula to our integral,</p>
<p>\(u=\) [[input:ans1a]] [[validation:ans1a]][[feedback:prt1a]]</p>
<p>\(dv=\) [[input:ans1b]] [[validation:ans1b]][[feedback:prt1b]]</p>
<p>so that \(v=\) [[input:ans1c]] [[validation:ans1c]][[feedback:prt1c]]</p>
<p>Now \(uv=\)[[input:ans1d]] [[validation:ans1d]][[feedback:prt1d]]</p>
<p>and \(\int vdu=\)[[input:ans1e]] [[validation:ans1e]][[feedback:prt1e]] </p> 
<p> Finally the integral \( \displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} dx = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked out solution</h3>
<p> The first step here is to pick \(u\) and \(dv\) so that when we compute \(du\) and \(v\) and plugging everything to have: </p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int udv = u v - \int vdu\)</p>
<p>With that in mind it looks like the following choices for \(u\) and \(dv\) should work for us: </p>
<p>\(u = {@func_var_u@}\), \(dv ={@func_var_dv@} dx\), therefore, <br> Next, we need to compute \(du\) (by differentiating \(u\)) and \(v\) (by integrating \(dv\)).</p>
<p>\(du = \displaystyle {@diff_u@}\, dx\) and \(v= \displaystyle {@int_v@}\)</p>

<p> Plugging the above into the Integration by Parts formula gives:</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} dx = ({@func_var_u@})({@int_v@}) - \displaystyle \int ({@int_v@}){@diff_u@} \,dx\)</p> <br>
<p> The new integral we get is easily doable, let's focus on it as shown:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int ({@int_v@}){@diff_u@} \,dx= {@int_v_du@} + c\), therefore replacing it back we’ll get:</p>
<p></p>
<p>\( \begin{align}
\displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} \, dx & =  ({@func_var_u@})\cdot({@int_v@}) - \displaystyle \int ({@int_v@}){@diff_u@} dx \\
& = {@ta@}
\end{align}\)</p>
<p>where \(c\) is constant of integration.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
